bounded k=4 weak-removal pressure test: last=None, states=250001, capped=True

FIRST-BOIL FRONTIER GROWTH
--------------------------
weak two-term k=3 frontiers: n=5:27, n=10:920, n=15:2929, n=20:357, n=23:3, n=24:0
weak two-term k=4 frontiers: n=5:37, n=10:12995, n=12:108640, n=13:250001+
k=4 crossed the 250000 frontier cap already at n=13; no last-boil value claimed.

ADVERSARIAL ATTACK-ONLY GAME
----------------------------
Rule: on odd n, A places n into one of B's cauldrons; on even n, B places n into one of A's.
Terminal: if your cauldrons are all boiled, you lose.
two-term cauldron (distinct, terms 2..2):
  A=1,B=1: winner=B, at_n=6, states=7
  A=1,B=2: winner=B, at_n=6, states=15
  A=2,B=1: winner=B, at_n=14, states=164
  A=2,B=2: winner=B, at_n=14, states=14633
two-or-three-term cauldron (distinct, terms 2..3):
  A=1,B=1: winner=B, at_n=6, states=7
  A=1,B=2: winner=B, at_n=6, states=15
  A=2,B=1: winner=A, at_n=9, states=30
  A=2,B=2: winner=B, at_n=14, states=9632
finite-sums cauldron (distinct, terms 2..unbounded):
  A=1,B=1: winner=B, at_n=6, states=7
  A=1,B=2: winner=B, at_n=6, states=15
  A=2,B=1: winner=A, at_n=9, states=30
  A=2,B=2: winner=B, at_n=14, states=9632
Reading: with two-term sums, A attacks only with odd numbers and cannot make
  an odd target from odd+odd, while B's even stream can make 2+4=6 and beyond.
  Allowing three/finite terms lets A use 1+3+5=9, flipping the asymmetric 2-vs-1 case.

VARIANT ATLAS / TOURNAMENT ANALYSIS
-----------------------------------
Vertices: cauldron variants and proof routes.
Pairwise observable: weighted score = 2*repo_connection + 2*proof_payload +
  difficulty + novelty + computability; ties use shorter certificate names.
Switch/gauge: prefer variants that retain side-channel payload while still being computable.
Score histogram: {0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1, 5: 1, 6: 1, 7: 1, 8: 1, 9: 1, 10: 1, 11: 1}
Directed 3-cycles: 0
Hamiltonian-path count: 1
Ranking:
   1. attack-only adversarial parity cauldrons (score=34, log10 score=1.53)
      turn order splits odd/even attack streams; a correlated residue can beat density
   2. Collatz residue cauldrons (score=31, log10 score=1.49)
      ingredients are 2/3 residue blocks; boil on correlated blind-density residues
   3. LRC depth cauldrons (score=31, log10 score=1.49)
      a cauldron boils when its danger arcs cover the observer circle, p0=0
   4. gift-or-poison adversarial cauldrons (score=31, log10 score=1.49)
      players may place n into own or opponent cauldrons; PSPACE-style finite game
   5. modular CRT cauldrons (score=31, log10 score=1.49)
      cauldrons live in Z/mZ and boil on zero-sum/subset-sum residues
   6. unit-distance geometric cauldrons (score=31, log10 score=1.49)
      points are placed into cauldrons; boil on unit edges/triangles or unfaithful embeddings
   7. OCF odd-cycle cauldrons (score=30, log10 score=1.48)
      odd cycles are ingredients; boil on forbidden independence-polynomial values
   8. hidden-quotient A000568 cauldrons (score=28, log10 score=1.45)
      state is observed only up to relabelling/orbit type, forcing Burnside-style side data
   9. upper-cap certificate cauldrons (score=28, log10 score=1.45)
      a boil creates a reusable certificate bounding all later safe placements
  10. finite-sums frontier cauldrons (score=27, log10 score=1.43)
      pair sums become subset-sum bitmasks, an all-orders overlap toy model
  11. misere cauldrons (score=24, log10 score=1.38)
      players try to force their own cauldrons to boil last/first under reversed payoff
  12. base last-boil Schur removal (score=23, log10 score=1.36)
      finite active-state DP over unlabeled sum-free color classes

